From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 00:38:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] media: rkvdec: map the RCB SRAM through the DMA API

The RCB buffers come out of an SRAM gen_pool, and the decoder reaches
them through the same IOMMU as everything else, so they have to be
mapped into its address space. That mapping is made by hand: iommu_map()
is called on rkvdec->iommu_domain with the SRAM's kernel virtual address
as the IOVA, and the registers are then programmed with that same value.

Two things are wrong with choosing the address that way.

rkvdec->iommu_domain is iommu_get_domain_for_dev(), which is the device's
DMA default domain -- the one whose IOVA allocator serves every
dma_alloc_coherent() on the device, including the vb2 capture buffers.
Nothing tells that allocator the RCB range is taken, so it is free to
hand the same addresses out again.

And rockchip-iommu builds its descriptor indices by masking the IOVA and
casting to u32, with no aperture check, so a 64-bit kernel virtual
address silently aliases to its low 32 bits. The address that collides is
not the one the driver picked.

The two together are a decode failure that waits for load: once
cumulative demand walks the IOVA allocator down onto the aliased range,
rk_iommu_map_iova() finds a live PTE and refuses, and the capture buffer
allocation fails:

  iova: 0x0000000080780000 already mapped to 0x000000003ff88000 \
    cannot remap to phys: 0x0000000183ff4000 prot: 0x3
  rkvdec 27b00000.video-codec: dma alloc of size 12443648 failed

0x3ff88000 there is the SRAM's own physical base, and 0x80780000 is what
its kernel virtual address masks down to. Size, bit depth and codec are
incidental; what reaches the collision is cumulative IOVA demand in the
one domain, so concurrent decoding finds it first.

Map the pool with dma_map_resource() instead. The IOVA then comes from
the same allocator as everything else on the device, which is what makes
it exclusive, and the reserved range is released with dma_unmap_resource()
from the free path.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-rcb.c     | 34 +++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-rcb.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-rcb.c
index fdcf1f177..ba23ab016 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-rcb.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvdec/rkvdec-rcb.c
@@ -9,8 +9,9 @@
 #include "rkvdec.h"
 #include "rkvdec-rcb.h"
 
-#include <linux/iommu.h>
+#include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 #include <linux/genalloc.h>
+#include <linux/iommu.h>
 #include <linux/sizes.h>
 #include <linux/types.h>
 
@@ -61,7 +62,10 @@ void rkvdec_free_rcb(struct rkvdec_ctx *ctx)
 			virt_addr = (unsigned long)cfg->rcb_bufs[i].cpu;
 
 			if (dev->iommu_domain)
-				iommu_unmap(dev->iommu_domain, virt_addr, rcb_size);
+				dma_unmap_resource(dev->dev,
+						   cfg->rcb_bufs[i].dma,
+						   rcb_size, DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL,
+						   0);
 			gen_pool_free(dev->sram_pool, virt_addr, rcb_size);
 			break;
 		case RKVDEC_ALLOC_DMA:
@@ -125,14 +129,22 @@ int rkvdec_allocate_rcb(struct rkvdec_ctx *ctx,
 							SZ_4K);
 		}
 
-		/* If an IOMMU is used, map the SRAM address through it */
+		/*
+		 * If an IOMMU is used, the SRAM has to be mapped into the
+		 * decoder's address space.  Go through the DMA API rather than
+		 * calling iommu_map() directly: the domain here is the device's
+		 * DMA domain, so an address chosen by hand is one the IOVA
+		 * allocator does not know is taken and will hand out again to a
+		 * frame buffer, and rockchip-iommu truncates an IOVA to 32 bits
+		 * without complaint, so the collision need not even be visible
+		 * in the address that was picked.
+		 */
 		if (cpu && rkvdec->iommu_domain) {
-			unsigned long virt_addr = (unsigned long)cpu;
-			phys_addr_t phys_addr = dma;
+			dma_addr_t iova = dma_map_resource(rkvdec->dev, dma,
+							   rcb_size,
+							   DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL, 0);
 
-			ret = iommu_map(rkvdec->iommu_domain, virt_addr, phys_addr,
-					rcb_size, IOMMU_READ | IOMMU_WRITE, 0);
-			if (ret) {
+			if (dma_mapping_error(rkvdec->dev, iova)) {
 				gen_pool_free(ctx->dev->sram_pool,
 					      (unsigned long)cpu,
 					      rcb_size);
@@ -141,10 +153,10 @@ int rkvdec_allocate_rcb(struct rkvdec_ctx *ctx,
 			}
 
 			/*
-			 * The registers will be configured with the virtual
-			 * address so that it goes through the IOMMU
+			 * The registers are configured with the mapped address
+			 * so that the access goes through the IOMMU.
 			 */
-			dma = virt_addr;
+			dma = iova;
 		}
 
 ram_fallback:
-- 
2.43.0

